## Write your section file, and only it
Your items in `items.json` order, each `\begin{<kind>}[id=<id>]{<title>}` with the item's title copied as it stands.

Write each statement as a good textbook prints it, and exactly as faithfully as the signature demands: those are one task, not two (`informal/STYLE.md`, "Statements: exactly the Lean, in a mathematician's words"). Use the standard term for a standard notion and the conventions' notation for it; do not repeat what `sections/00-conventions.tex` has fixed -- the ambient objects, the typeclass and universe binders, the index type of a family, the coercions, the side conditions the library discharges inside a statement. A term may carry a hypothesis only when it says exactly that hypothesis, never more (no "open cover" where the Lean has neighborhoods); where it would say more, write the hypothesis. Of two statements that say the same mathematics, write the shorter.

For each:
- the statement, faithful to the elaborated signature: every hypothesis, the order of the quantifiers, ℕ versus ℤ, the coercions; a standing hypothesis through its macro of `notation.sty`;
- one `\itempart{<letter>}{...}` per part, in order;
- `\leanref{<full name>}` for every key declaration, in its part (or in the item's own text when its part is null), and for a helper only where a reader wants it;
- a sentence for each junk value the item lists;
- `\fcref{<number>}` where `items.json` gives the item or the part a reference number, `\fcref*{<number>}` when its source is `inferred`, once;
- at most a sentence or two of connecting prose between items; no proofs.
The file writes no `\section`: `sections.tex` does.

